From: lipemat/wp-phpcs
Subject: Enable --parallel on Windows via proc_open fallback
PHPCS's Runner only parallelizes via pcntl_fork, which is unavailable on
Windows PHP builds. This patch adds a proc_open-based strategy that is
selected automatically when pcntl is missing. The pcntl path is left
untouched so POSIX behaviour is unchanged.
Each batch is launched with proc_open, with the child's stdout/stderr
redirected to temp FILES (not pipes) to avoid the Windows pipe-buffer
deadlock, and the file list passed via --file-list. The child writes a
JSON report to --report-file; the parent merges each report into its
Reporter via DummyFile so all configured report formats receive the
violations, and prints one progress dot per file (reflecting E/W/S).
Caching: when --cache is enabled, each child is seeded with the parent's
cache file and writes to its own per-shard cache; the parent merges all
child cache entries back before Cache::save(), so consecutive runs are
fast. Pass --no-cache to disable.
Diagnostic: set PHPCS_BATCH_TIMING=1 to print the slowest 7 batches
(file lists + wall-clock elapsed) to STDERR after the run. Override the
count via PHPCS_BATCH_TIMING_TOP=N.
--- a/src/Runner.php
+++ b/src/Runner.php
@@ -429,8 +429,10 @@ class Runner
             $this->config->parallel = 1;
         }
 
-        // If the PCNTL extension isn't installed, we can't fork.
-        if (function_exists('pcntl_fork') === false) {
+        // Determine which parallelism strategy is available on this platform.
+        $canPcntl    = function_exists('pcntl_fork');
+        $canProcOpen = (function_exists('proc_open') === true && function_exists('proc_get_status') === true);
+        if ($canPcntl === false && $canProcOpen === false) {
             $this->config->parallel = 1;
         }
 
